WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:08.892
Jordan Lee: Okay, I think we're recording. Thanks for making time, Aisha. This is the Harbor Freightways review. Congrats, by the way.

2
00:00:09.592 --> 00:00:14.208
Aisha Okafor: Thank you! Yeah, this one felt good. Happy to walk through it.

3
00:00:14.908 --> 00:00:21.254
Jordan Lee: Great. Same format as always. I'll ask a handful of questions, and "I don't know" is a totally fine answer.

4
00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:27.600
Jordan Lee: Let's start at the beginning. What happened at Harbor that started this evaluation?

5
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:35.479
Aisha Okafor: So the short version is they got dinged on their audit. Their external auditors flagged a material weakness on expense approvals.

6
00:00:35.479 --> 00:00:42.659
Aisha Okafor: Basically there was no real evidence that anyone was approving spend before it went out the door.

7
00:00:42.659 --> 00:00:49.838
Aisha Okafor: That landed on the desk of their new CFO, Dev Patel, who'd only been there maybe six months.

8
00:00:50.538 --> 00:00:58.231
Jordan Lee: And how did you find out about the audit finding? Did they tell you, or did you piece it together?

9
00:00:58.931 --> 00:01:05.982
Aisha Okafor: They told me. On the first call the controller, Nicole Brandt, said it pretty plainly.

10
00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:13.033
Aisha Okafor: She said, "We're approving expenses on the honor system, and the auditors noticed." And then she said Dev had committed to the audit committee that it'd be fixed before the next audit cycle.

11
00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:20.085
Aisha Okafor: So there was a clock on it.

12
00:01:20.785 --> 00:01:25.400
Jordan Lee: That's really helpful. Do you know when the next audit cycle is?

13
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:34.177
Aisha Okafor: Fieldwork starts in March. So they needed to be live and have a couple of months of clean data before then.

14
00:01:34.877 --> 00:01:39.492
Jordan Lee: Got it. Who made the final call, and how do you know?

15
00:01:40.192 --> 00:01:52.115
Aisha Okafor: Dev. The CFO. I'm confident on that one because he said it on the final call.

16
00:01:52.115 --> 00:02:04.038
Aisha Okafor: Nicole was our champion, she ran the whole evaluation, but on the last call Dev said, and I'm paraphrasing a little, "Nicole's done the work, but this one's my call and I'm making it." And then he signed the order form himself, like, two days later.

17
00:02:04.738 --> 00:02:07.815
Jordan Lee: Love that. Was anyone else involved? IT, procurement?

18
00:02:08.515 --> 00:02:12.746
Aisha Okafor: IT did a security review.

19
00:02:12.746 --> 00:02:16.977
Aisha Okafor: They asked for our SOC 1 Type II and our SOC 2, and we had both, so that was quick.

20
00:02:16.977 --> 00:02:21.208
Aisha Okafor: Procurement wasn't really a factor at their size.

21
00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:25.754
Jordan Lee: Okay. What did the customer bring up about the competitor?

22
00:02:26.454 --> 00:02:37.031
Aisha Okafor: Spendgrid was the other one in. Nicole brought them up on the second call, said they'd also be seeing Spendgrid.

23
00:02:37.031 --> 00:02:47.608
Aisha Okafor: And after the Spendgrid demo she told me, and this is a direct quote because I wrote it in my notes, "Spendgrid's demo felt like it was built for a company ten times our size."

24
00:02:48.308 --> 00:02:53.308
Jordan Lee: That's a great quote. Did you hear anything else about Spendgrid, like pricing?

25
00:02:54.008 --> 00:02:58.815
Aisha Okafor: Not really pricing.

26
00:02:58.815 --> 00:03:03.623
Aisha Okafor: I'd guess Spendgrid was more expensive, they usually are, but nobody told me a number, so I wouldn't put weight on that.

27
00:03:04.323 --> 00:03:08.554
Jordan Lee: Fair. What changed in their questions after they'd met with Spendgrid?

28
00:03:09.254 --> 00:03:15.888
Aisha Okafor: Oh, that's a good one. Before Spendgrid, the questions were mostly about approval workflows.

29
00:03:15.888 --> 00:03:22.523
Aisha Okafor: How do policies work, can we route by amount. After Spendgrid, Nicole started asking a lot about implementation.

30
00:03:22.523 --> 00:03:29.158
Aisha Okafor: How long does it take, how many admins do we need, who on our side has to do the work.

31
00:03:29.158 --> 00:03:35.792
Aisha Okafor: I think Spendgrid's implementation plan scared her a bit. That's my read, though. She didn't say that.

32
00:03:36.492 --> 00:03:43.031
Jordan Lee: Got it, that's your read. Which part of our pitch got a reaction, and which got silence?

33
00:03:43.731 --> 00:03:48.859
Aisha Okafor: The audit trail. Hands down.

34
00:03:48.859 --> 00:03:53.987
Aisha Okafor: When I showed the approval history, like who approved what, when, with the policy attached, Dev leaned in.

35
00:03:53.987 --> 00:03:59.115
Aisha Okafor: He said, "That's the screen I'm going to show the audit committee." That was the moment, honestly.

36
00:03:59.815 --> 00:04:01.354
Jordan Lee: And what got silence?

37
00:04:02.054 --> 00:04:09.490
Aisha Okafor: The real-time dashboards. I did my usual spend visibility section, the live budget view, and they just kind of nodded.

38
00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:16.926
Aisha Okafor: Nobody asked a single question. I don't think they cared about visibility, they cared about control.

39
00:04:16.926 --> 00:04:24.362
Aisha Okafor: The close-faster stuff got a little interest from Nicole, she asked a couple of questions about reconciliations, but it wasn't driving anything.

40
00:04:25.062 --> 00:04:30.831
Jordan Lee: That's really useful. When did you first feel this was won? What happened right before?

41
00:04:31.531 --> 00:04:38.582
Aisha Okafor: Right after the audit trail demo with Dev. That was the third meeting.

42
00:04:38.582 --> 00:04:45.633
Aisha Okafor: When I finished that section he asked, "What would this cost us for next year, and can we be live by January?" Before that he'd been kind of quiet, arms crossed.

43
00:04:45.633 --> 00:04:52.685
Aisha Okafor: After that it felt like the question was when, not if.

44
00:04:53.385 --> 00:04:58.769
Jordan Lee: So if you had to say the real reason we won, what is it?

45
00:04:59.469 --> 00:05:04.469
Aisha Okafor: We fixed his audit problem and we looked like we could do it in time. Spendgrid looked like a bigger, slower project.

46
00:05:04.469 --> 00:05:09.469
Aisha Okafor: I think that's it.

47
00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:13.631
Jordan Lee: What did they ask for that we couldn't show?

48
00:05:14.331 --> 00:05:24.908
Aisha Okafor: A reference in logistics. Dev asked to talk to another trucking or logistics company using us, and we didn't have one.

49
00:05:24.908 --> 00:05:35.485
Aisha Okafor: I gave him a manufacturing customer instead, Ridgeway Components, and it was fine, but he did say, "It's not the same, our drivers are spending money all over the country." So that's a gap.

50
00:05:36.185 --> 00:05:37.685
Jordan Lee: Anything else?

51
00:05:38.385 --> 00:05:43.385
Aisha Okafor: No, I think that was the main one. The SOC reports we had.

52
00:05:44.085 --> 00:05:48.315
Jordan Lee: Last one. What exact words did they use for their problem?

53
00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:53.887
Aisha Okafor: The honor system line from Nicole.

54
00:05:53.887 --> 00:05:58.759
Aisha Okafor: "We're approving expenses on the honor system." And Dev said something on the final call that stuck with me.

55
00:05:58.759 --> 00:06:03.631
Aisha Okafor: He said, "I need to stop finding out about problems from the auditors."

56
00:06:04.331 --> 00:06:07.792
Jordan Lee: Perfect. Anything I didn't ask that I should have?

57
00:06:08.492 --> 00:06:13.877
Aisha Okafor: Maybe just that the drivers thing is real. A lot of their spend is fuel and repairs on the road.

58
00:06:13.877 --> 00:06:19.262
Aisha Okafor: We should have a better story for that.

59
00:06:19.962 --> 00:06:22.269
Jordan Lee: Noted. Thanks, Aisha, this was great.
